INFO:agent.loop:\u26a1 Executing tool: 'knowledge_agent' with arguments: {"source_title": "DOJ indictment and Senate/technical reports on IRA 2016 operation", "source_url": "SRC-001; SRC-003; SRC-004; SRC-002", "text": "Evidence returned by benchmark tools: DOJ indictment SRC-001 alleges IRA LLC was a Russian organization conducting election/political interference, operating from St. Petersburg and through aliases Internet Research LLC, MediaSintez, GlavSet, MixInfo, Azimut, NovInfo. It alleges operations began by 2014, with false U.S. personas and stolen identities managing social-media pages/groups aimed at U.S. audiences; U.S.-based infrastructure and travel under false pretenses were used to conceal origin and gather intelligence. The indictment alleges a strategic goal to sow discord; by early/mid-2016 operations supported Donald Trump and disparaged Hillary Clinton, bought ads in U.S. names, staged rallies, solicited/compensated U.S. persons, and contacted unwitting Trump-associated individuals. The translator project operated on YouTube, Facebook, Instagram, Twitter; >80 employees by July 2016; stated goal in May 2014 to spread distrust toward candidates and political system. Funding: Yevgeniy Prigozhin controlled Concord Management and Consulting and Concord Catering; Concord was primary funder, oversaw operations/personnel; Project Lakhta targeted domestic and foreign audiences; Sept 2016 monthly budget exceeded 73m rubles (> $1.25m), funds disguised as software support and routed through about 14 affiliate bank accounts. Senate Volume II SRC-003 finds IRA operatives used social media in 2016 for disinformation and societal division, masquerading as Americans, targeted ads, falsified news, self-generated content and platform tools, reaching/attempting to deceive tens of millions. Findings: IRA sought to harm Clinton and support Trump at Kremlin direction; campaign objectives extended beyond election to undermine trust and sow discord; preponderance focused on race, immigration, Second Amendment, stoking anger, outrage, protest and distrust. African Americans were the most targeted group; >66% of Facebook ads had race-related terms, Blacktivist generated 11.2m Facebook engagements, five of top ten Instagram accounts focused on African-American issues, 96% of YouTube content targeted racial issues/police brutality. Paid ads were not the main activity: about $100k ads over two years vs approximately $1.25m monthly operating costs; nearly 3,400 Facebook/Instagram ads compared with >61,500 Facebook posts, 116,000 Instagram posts and 10.4m tweets. IRA also coopted unwitting Americans for petitions, personal information, self-defense courses, and rally support/materials. Senate says Kremlin tasked/supported IRA; Prigozhin's direction and ties to high-level Russian officials point to significant Kremlin support/authorization/direction, while full scope remains unknown because committee relied on platform companies. SRC-004 technical report based on platform data says 2013-2018 campaigns reached tens of millions; >30m users shared IRA Facebook/Instagram posts 2015-17; activity peaks tracked political/crisis dates; strategy evolved from Twitter to Facebook/Instagram/YouTube; organic posting had greater reach than ads. It assesses goals included urging African-American voters to boycott elections or follow wrong procedures, encouraging extreme right-wing confrontation, and spreading sensationalist/conspiratorial/junk news across political spectrum; activity increased after election and continued."}
